latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Find the gray line:\n   - Iterate through the rows of the grid\n   - Identify the row where all cells are gray (color 5)\n   - Store this row index as `gray_line_index`\n\n2. Initialize data structures:\n   - Create dictionaries for `above_counts`, `below_counts`, and `rightmost_positions`\n   - Initialize variables `total_above` and `total_below` to 0\n\n3. Scan the grid:\n   - Iterate through each cell in the grid\n   - If the color is not black (0) or gray (5):\n     - If the row is above `gray_line_index`:\n       - Increment the color's count in `above_counts`\n       - Increment `total_above`\n     - If the row is below `gray_line_index`:\n       - Increment the color's count in `below_counts`\n       - Increment `total_below`\n     - Update the `rightmost_positions` for this color\n\n4. Calculate significance scores:\n   - For each color that appears both above and below the gray line:\n     - Calculate score = (below_count / total_below) - (above_count / total_above)\n     - Store the score along with the color and its rightmost position\n\n5. Determine the most significant color:\n   - Sort the colors based on:\n     1. Highest significance score\n     2. If scores are tied, use the rightmost position as a tie-breaker\n   - Select the first color after sorting\n\n6. Handle edge case:\n   - If no colors were found (other than black and gray), set the chosen color to black (0)\n\n7. Create the output grid:\n   - Generate a 2x2 grid filled with the chosen color\n\n8. Return the output grid\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects we identified in our reflection:\n- It considers the relative distribution of colors above and below the gray line\n- It uses the rightmost position as a tie-breaker\n- It works regardless of the gray line's position or the grid's size\n- It handles the edge case where no significant colors are present\n\nBy following this plan, we should be able to correctly solve all the given examples and generalize to other potential inputs within the problem's constraints."
